{{smaller|receiving-instrument, the object being to obtain a strong current to work the sounder or recorder.—'''Energy of translation, equation of translation, principle of translation, surface of translation.''' See ''energy'', etc.—'''Translation of a feast''', the postponement to some future day of the observance of a feast, when the day of its ordinary observance falls upon a festival of superior rank.=Syn. 1. (''d'') ''Translation, Version'', rendering. ''Translation'' and ''version'' are often the same in meaning. ''Translation'' is rather the standard word. ''Version'' is more likely to be employed in proportion to the antiquity of the work: as, the Syriac ''version''; Dryden's ''version'' of the Nun's Priest's Tale; it is also more commonly used of the Bible than of other books: as, a comparison of the authorized with the revised ''version''. Where ''translations'' differ, they are often spoken of as ''versions'', as Lord Derby's and Mr. Bryant's ''translations'' or ''versions'' of Homer. ''Version'' applies more to the meaning, ''translation'' more to the style. Each has meanings not shared by the other.}}

'''translational''' (trȧns-lā′sho̤n-a̤l), ''a.'' [< ''translation'' + ''-al''.] Pertaining to or having the character of translation. See ''translation'', 6.

{{smaller block|The whole ''translational'' energy . . . must ultimately become transformed . . . into vibrational energy.
{{right|''Philos. Mag.'', 5th ser., XXX. clxxxii. 95.}}}}

'''translatitious†''' (trȧns-lā-tish′us), ''a.'' [< L. ''translaticius, translatitius'', handed down, transmitted, hereditary, < ''translatus'', pp. of ''transferre'', transfer, translate: see ''translate''. Cf. ''tralatitious''.] 1. Transmitted; transferred; hereditary.

{{smaller block|I have frequently doubted whether it be a pure indigene, or ''translatitious''.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Sylva, I. iv. § 8.}}}}

2. Same as ''tralatitious''.

{{smaller block|A delegated ''translatitious'' Majesty we allow.
{{right|''Milton'', Answer to Salmasius, vii. 179.}}}}

'''translative''' (trȧns′lā-tiv), ''a.'' [= Sp. ''translativo, traslativo'' = It. ''traslativo''; as ''translate'' +

readings.c1s2:
-''ive.''] Relating or pertaining to translation; especially, involving transference of meaning; metaphorical. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|If our feete Poeticall want these qualities, it can not be sayd a foote in sence ''translatiue'' as here.
{{right|''Puttenham'', Arte of Eng. Poesie, p. 56.}}}}

'''translator''' (trȧns-lā′tọr), ''n.'' [= F. ''translateur'' = It. ''traslatore'' (cf. Sp. Pg. ''trasladador'' = It. ''traslatatore''), < L. ''translator'', one who transfers or interprets, < ''translatus'', pp. of ''transferre'', transfer, translate: see ''translate''.] One who or that which translates.

{{smaller block|The changer and ''translator'' of kyngedoms and tymes.
{{right|''Joye'', Expos. of Daniel, v.}}}}

{{smaller block|Specifically—(''a'') One who renders something spoken or written in one language into another: as, he held the office of public ''translator''.}}

{{smaller block|A noble author would not be pursued too close by a ''translator''. We lose his spirit when we think to take his body.
{{right|''Dryden'', Orig. and Prog. of Satire.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''b'') A cobbler of a low class, who manufactures boots and shoes from the material of old ones, selling them at a low price to second-hand dealers. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|The cobbler is affronted if you don't call him Mr. ''Translator''.
{{right|''Tom Brown'', Works, III. 73. (''Davies.'')}}}}

{{smaller block|(''c'') ''pl.'' Second-hand boots mended and sold at a low price. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|A costermonger . . . will part with everything rather than his boots, and to wear a pair of second-hand ones, or ''translators'' (as they are called), is felt as a bitter degradation by them all.
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, I. 51.}}}}

{{smaller block|(''d'') In ''teleg.'', a sensitive receiving-instrument used for retransmitting a message, or for translation: commonly}}

readings.c1s3:
called a ''relay''. (''e'') Any instrument for converting one form of energy into another: thus, the magneto-electric engine which transforms the power of a steam-engine into electricity is a ''translator''.

'''translatory''' (trȧns′lạ̄-tọ̄-ri), ''a''. [< ''translate'' + ''-ory''.] 1. Transferring; serving to translate.

{{smaller block|The ''translatory'' is a lie that transfers the merits of a man's good action to another more deserving. {{right|''Arbuthnot.''}}}}

2. Same as ''translational''.

{{smaller block|The ''translatory'' velocity of the whirlwind itself.
{{right|''The Atlantic'', XLIX. 331.}}}}

'''translatress''' (trȧns-lā′tres), ''n''. [< ''translator'' + ''-ess''.] A woman who translates, in any sense of that word.

{{smaller block|Your great Achilles, Cardinal Perron (in French; as also his noble ''Translatress'', misled by him, in English), . . . hath made bold with the Latin tongue.
{{right|''Chillingworth'', Religion of Protestants, I. vi. § 29.}}}}

'''translavation†''' (trȧns-lạ̄-vā′shọn), ''n''. [< L. ''trans'', over, + ''lavatio(n-)'', a washing: see ''lave''<sup>1</sup>.] A laving or lading from one vessel into another.

{{smaller block|This ''translavation'' ought so long to be continued out of one vessell into another, untill such time as it have done casting any residence downward.
{{right|''Holland'', tr. of Pliny, xxxiv. 18.}}}}

'''transleithan''' (trȧns-lī′than), ''a''. [< ''trans-'' + ''Leitha'' (see def.) + ''-an''.] Beyond the Leitha, a river flowing partly along the boundary between Hungary and the archduchy of Austria: noting that division of the empire of Austria-Hungary which has its seat in Budapest. Compare ''Austrian''<sup>1</sup>.

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''transliterate''' (trȧns-lit′e̤-rāt), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''transliterated'', ppr. ''transliterating''. [< L. ''trans'', over, + ''litera'', letter: see ''letter''<sup>3</sup>, ''literate''.] To express or write, as words of a language having peculiar alphabetic characters, in the alphabetic characters of another language; spell (the same, or approximately the same, sound) in different characters.

{{smaller block|Greek names ''transliterated'' into a Latin alphabet are subject to the laws of Latin phonology.
{{right|''Isaac Taylor'', The Alphabet, I., Pref., p. ix.}}}}

'''transliteration''' (trȧns-lit-e̤-rā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''transliterate'' + ''-ion''.] The act of transliterating; the rendering of a letter or letters of one alphabet by equivalents in another.

{{smaller block|The ''transliteration'' does not profess to give all the exact vocalic differences. ''The Academy'', June 28, 1890, p. 448.}}

'''transliterator''' (trȧns-lit′e̤-rā-to̤r), ''n.'' [< ''transliterate'' + ''-or''<sup>1</sup>.] One who transliterates; one who makes a transliteration.

{{smaller block|It seems to have been the object of the ''transliterator'' to represent, at least approximately, in Anglo-Saxon letters the current pronunciation of the Greek words.
{{right|''J. Hadley'', Essays, p. 128.}}}}

'''translocalization''' (trȧns-lō″kal-i-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''trans-'' + ''localization''.] Same as ''translocation''.

{{smaller block|Patients often unfold a train of reminiscence extempore upon any theme, and sometimes cannot repeat the same pseudo-experience twice alike, ''translocalizations'' in time being especially common. ''Amer. Jour. Psychol.'', I. 538.}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|The subtle essence acted on him like a charmed draught, and caused the opaque substance of his animal being to grow transparent, or at least ''translucent''; so that a spiritual gleam was transmitted through it with a clearer lustre than hitherto.|{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, vii.}}}}

2. Transparent; clear.

{{smaller block|The golden ew'r a maid obsequious brings,<br>Replenish'd from the cool, ''translucent'' springs.|{{right|''Pope'', Odyssey, i. 180.}}}}

'''translucently''' (trȧns-lū′sẹnt-li), ''adv.'' In a translucent manner. ''Drayton'', Edward IV. to Mistress Shore, Annotation 3.

'''translucid''' (trȧns-lū′sid), ''a.'' [= F. ''translucide'' = Sp. ''traslucido'' = Pg. ''translucido'' = It. ''translucido, traslucido'', < L. ''translucidus, traslucidus'', shining through, < ''translucere'', shine through: see ''translucent''. Cf. ''lucid''.] Translucent.

{{smaller block|Flowers whose purple and ''translucid'' bowls<br>Stand ever mantling with aërial dew.|{{right|''Shelley'', Prometheus Unbound, iii. 3.}}}}

'''translunar''' (trȧns-lū′när), ''a.'' [< L. ''trans'', across, + ''luna'', moon: see ''lunar''.] Being beyond the moon: opposed to ''sublunary''. ''Drayton'', To Henry Reynolds.

'''translunary''' (trȧns′lụ̄-nạ̄-ri), ''a.'' Same as ''translunar''.

'''transmarine''' (trȧns-ma-rēn′), ''a.'' [< F. ''transmarin'' = Pg. ''transmariño'' = Sp. It. ''transmarino, trasmarino'', < L. ''transmarinus'', beyond or from beyond sea, < ''trans'', over, + ''mare'', sea, ''marinus'', of the sea, marine: see ''marine''.] Located or existing beyond the sea.

{{smaller block|Their Dutch appellations are really too hard<br>To be brought into verse by a ''transmarine'' Bard.|{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 19.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
change into another form: see ''transmute.'' Cf. ''mew''<sup>3</sup>.] To transmute; transform; metamorphose.

{{smaller block|Thow moost me ferst ''transmuwen'' in a stoon.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 467.}}}}

{{smaller block|Men into stones therewith he could ''transmew.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., I. vii. 35.}}}}

{{smaller block|To ''transmew'' thyself from a holy hermit into a sinful forester.
{{right|''Scott'', Ivanhoe, xx.}}}}

¶
'''transmigrant''' (trȧns′mi-grant), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''transmigran(t-)s'', ppr. of ''transmigrare'', transmigrate: see ''transmigrate.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Passing into another country or state for residence, or into another form or body; migrating. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' 1. One who migrates or leaves his own country and passes into another for settlement; a colonist.

{{smaller block|There are other . . . implicit confederations. That of colonies, or ''transmigrants'', towards their mother nation.
{{right|''Bacon'', Holy War.}}}}

2. One who passes into another state or body. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''transmigrate''' (trȧns′mi-grāt), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''transmigrated'', ppr. ''transmigrating.'' [< L. ''transmigratus'', pp. of ''transmigrare'' (> It. ''trasmigrare'' = Sp. ''transmigrar'', ''trasmigrar'' = F. ''transmigrer''), remove from one place to another, < ''trans'', over, + ''migrare'', depart, migrate: see ''migrate.'' Cf. ''emigrate'', ''immigrate.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' 1. To migrate; pass from one country or jurisdiction to another for the purpose of residing in it.

{{smaller block|This complexion . . . is evidently maintained by generation, . . . so that strangers contract it not, and the natives which ''transmigrate'' amit it, not without commixture.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., vi. 10.}}}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
2. To pass from one body into another; be transformed; specifically, to become incarnate in a different body; metempsychosize.

{{smaller block|It [the crocodile] lives by that which nourisheth it; and, the elements once out of it, it ''transmigrates''.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., ii. 7. 51.}}}}

{{smaller block|Rabbi Benjamin of Tudela records in the 12th century of the Druses of Mount Hermon: "They say that the soul of a virtuous man is transferred to the body of a new-born child, whereas that of the vicious ''transmigrates'' into a dog, or some other animal."
{{right|''E. B. Tylor'', Prim. Culture, II. 13.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To cause to pass or migrate from one region or state of existence to another.

{{smaller block|Excellent Spirits are not by Death extinguished or neglected, but are rather ''transmigrated'' from the earth, to reigne with the Powers aboue.
{{right|''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 463.}}}}

'''transmigration''' (trȧns-mi-grā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ME. ''transmigracioun'', < OF. (and F.) ''transmigration'' = Sp. ''transmigracion'', ''trasmigracion'' = Pg. ''transmigração'' = It. ''trasmigrazione'', < LL. ''transmigratio(n-)'', < L. ''transmigrare'', transmigrate: see ''transmigrate''.] The act of transmigrating; passage from one place, state, or form into another.

{{smaller block|Lately hath this peerlesse man [Isaac Casabonus] made a happy ''transmigration'' out of France into our renowned island of great Britaine.
{{right|''Coryat'', Crudities, I. 43.}}}}

{{smaller block|What see I on any side but the ''transmigrations'' of Proteus?
{{right|''Emerson'', History.}}}}

{{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') In ''physiol.'', the passage of cells through a membrane or the wall of a vessel: as, the ''transmigration''}}
